<string id="ui_sharper_guide_descr"><text>%c[gray]Before you start:\n%c[default]&#160;&#160;&#160;Don't reach for the stone until the knife has actually gone dull - it's a waste of good stone.\n \n%c[gray]What to rinse with:\n%c[default]&#160;&#160;&#160;• Vodka - best by far, the grime just lifts off.\n&#160;&#160;&#160;• Energy drink - will do if you're out of vodka.\n&#160;&#160;&#160;• Water - better than nothing.\n \n%c[gray]About the stone:\n%c[default]&#160;&#160;&#160;A clogged stone cuts worse, and ground-in grime never fully comes out. Rinse with whatever's at hand and it wears down faster. Your hand gets the knack over time: an experienced sharpener can get an edge out of an old stone.\n \n%c[green] (!)%c[255,128,128,128] Drag a knife or your rinsing stuff onto the stone. Or the other way around.%c[default]</text></string>
<string id="sharper_desc"><text>A hand sharpener with a pair of abrasive stones. It brings a knife's edge back. The stones clog with grime and wear down - sooner or later you'll need a new one.</text></string>
